WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:01.797
- Picture day was something

00:00:01.797 --> 00:00:03.179
that I actually kind of dreaded.

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:05.051
You have your picture taken that's the way

00:00:05.051 --> 00:00:07.421
it's gonna be in the yearbook, forever.

00:00:07.421 --> 00:00:09.797
Little did I know that
I was actually taking

00:00:09.797 --> 00:00:13.297
part in this ritual that I would research.

00:00:14.218 --> 00:00:17.051
(uplifting music)

00:00:19.389 --> 00:00:20.757
- [Announcer] What if
you could write a formula

00:00:20.757 --> 00:00:23.088
so advanced that you could
wade through millions

00:00:23.088 --> 00:00:26.317
of yearbook photos to track social trends.

00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:28.189
Researchers at UC Berkeley are helping

00:00:28.189 --> 00:00:30.248
historians do just that.

00:00:30.248 --> 00:00:32.008
- This project is really about history.

00:00:32.008 --> 00:00:33.429
We looked for a data set

00:00:33.429 --> 00:00:36.077
that would kind of capture changes

00:00:36.077 --> 00:00:37.917
in people and we found that

00:00:37.917 --> 00:00:39.757
we could use high school yearbooks

00:00:39.757 --> 00:00:42.406
because yearbooks have
existed in high schools

00:00:42.406 --> 00:00:45.379
in America from about 1902

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:48.691
and throughout this entire time the format

00:00:48.691 --> 00:00:50.800
has stayed exactly the same.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.811
You go to school and you dress up nice

00:00:52.811 --> 00:00:54.238
and you stand in front of the camera

00:00:54.238 --> 00:00:56.905
and you have your picture taken.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.869
So there's millions,
literally, of photographs

00:01:01.869 --> 00:01:04.536
that are basically all the same.

00:01:06.491 --> 00:01:08.429
One of the things we were able to create

00:01:08.429 --> 00:01:11.346
was an average image or a composite

00:01:12.531 --> 00:01:15.718
of what the average teen looked like

00:01:15.718 --> 00:01:18.079
in the 20th century.

00:01:18.079 --> 00:01:19.559
We could just measure, literally,

00:01:19.559 --> 00:01:21.859
the curvature of the smile.

00:01:21.859 --> 00:01:24.692
There's basically one big up curve

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.019
until the second world war and then there

00:01:28.019 --> 00:01:30.785
are a couple of dips
but it keeps going up.

00:01:30.785 --> 00:01:32.832
And for every decade we found

00:01:32.832 --> 00:01:34.312
a bunch of subgroups.

00:01:34.312 --> 00:01:36.979
In the 1960s, we found for girls

00:01:38.301 --> 00:01:39.800
all these different hairstyles.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.529
There's the flip, that goes like this.

00:01:41.529 --> 00:01:43.394
Then there's the beehive

00:01:43.394 --> 00:01:46.657
which is this big bubble thing.

00:01:46.657 --> 00:01:49.867
And then in the ‘70s we get the afros

00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:51.619
and the bouffants and all kinds

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:53.648
of stuff like that.

00:01:53.648 --> 00:01:55.667
The data kind of speaks to us.

00:01:55.667 --> 00:01:57.139
And things emerge from the data.

00:01:57.139 --> 00:01:59.069
All kinds of pattern or trend without

00:01:59.069 --> 00:02:01.080
needing any human annotation.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.139
And this is a really big deal because

00:02:03.139 --> 00:02:06.778
mostly big data is just
really a bunch of noise.

00:02:06.778 --> 00:02:09.537
If we can find ways to automatically tease

00:02:09.537 --> 00:02:12.539
these things out, we, as humans

00:02:12.539 --> 00:02:15.389
will have much less
work in order to be able

00:02:15.389 --> 00:02:18.389
to understand what we see.

00:02:18.389 --> 00:02:21.472
(enthusiastic music)

